/* ==========================================================================
   PerfLint's windows — the few things C# inline styles cannot express.

   Written for the Autopilot and now worn by all five windows. That is the
   point of the rename: the Autopilot was where this was worked out, not what
   it is for. Everything below is loaded through PerfLintStyle.Apply, which is
   also where the C# half of the same palette lives (Ink/Dim/Good/Bad/Amber —
   the tints a StyleSheet cannot reach because they are chosen per finding).

   Two of them, and both were missing until this file existed:

   1. THEME COLOURS. `var(--unity-colors-*)` resolves against whichever editor
      skin is running, so one value is correct on both. Hand-picked greys in C#
      are correct on exactly the skin they were eyeballed against — and this
      window's were picked on a dark 6.3, which is why it read as a foreign
      panel rather than an editor window.

   2. :hover / :active. There is no inline equivalent; a Button that sets its
      background in C# also loses the editor's own hover feedback, so every
      custom button in here was dead to the touch. Anything that sets those
      colours must therefore do it HERE, not inline, or the inline value wins
      and the pseudo-states never paint.

   Tokens follow the reference plugin Tim set as the bar (com.ivanmurzak.unity.mcp,
   Apache-2.0, Editor/UI/uss/) — its numbers, our semantics. Nothing is copied
   from it verbatim: what carries over is which properties do the work, and the
   finding that a card should be tinted by its STATE rather than left neutral.
   ========================================================================== */

/* ── surfaces ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */

/* The window is the editor's window. Measured against the reference side by side: it runs on Unity's own
   #383838, with content sitting straight on it; this window ran on #2A2B2E with a #45464A slab covering the
   whole screen underneath everything — two layers of neutral grey before any content. That slab is what read
   as "grey". It carried no meaning: the step strip above it already says where the screen begins. */
.pl-window {
    background-color: var(--unity-colors-window-background);
}

/* A block of supporting detail. Their treatment, and it is the opposite of what this window did: a nested
   panel goes DARKER than what it sits on (and picks up a trace of blue), rather than lighter — lighter reads
   as raised, i.e. as something you could click. */
.pl-panel {
    background-color: rgba(20, 40, 69, 0.2);
}

/* A card, and it has to be VISIBLE as one.
 *
 * It used to be `var(--unity-colors-helpbox-background)` — the theme's own surface, which is the right instinct and
 * measured wrong: on the dark skin that resolves to #404040 against a #383838 window, which is 1.13:1. Sampled off
 * a real capture rather than argued, and 1.13 is nothing — the block does not read as a block, so a screen that
 * puts half its text in cards and half loose looks like one flat grey either way. Tim, on the shader panel: "这个字
 * 的底图是否要换个颜色".
 *
 * Two changes, and the second is what the first alone could not do:
 *
 *  · The FILL lifts to ~#4A4A4A on the dark skin (1.32:1) — enough to see, deliberately short of the ~1.5 that
 *    starts to read as RAISED, i.e. as something you could click. That distinction is the whole reason .pl-panel
 *    goes darker rather than lighter.
 *
 *  · A HAIRLINE, which is the part that draws the edge. It contradicts what this file used to say — "a neutral one
 *    does not need it, and outlining every block is what made these panels read as a stack of boxes" — and that
 *    sentence was written when the fill was assumed to be doing the work. At 1.13:1 it was not doing any.
 *
 * NEUTRAL GREY rather than a fixed colour or a white overlay, for the same reason .pl-secondary is (see its note):
 * white only ever lightens, so a value picked on the dark skin lightens an already-light page on the other one.
 * 127-grey lifts a dark surface and darkens a light one — over #383838 it lands on 74 (#4A4A4A), and over the light
 * skin's ~#C8C8C8 it lands on ~#B6B6B6, a card that is visible there too. One value, correct on both. */
.pl-card {
    background-color: rgba(127, 127, 127, 0.25);
    border-width: 1px;
    border-color: rgba(127, 127, 127, 0.35);
    transition-duration: 150ms;
}

/* A tinted block is a DARK fill of its hue, plus a rule of the same hue one step brighter.

   Both halves were wrong before and both were measured off the reference. Its blocks sit BELOW the window
   grey, not above it: the panel behind "Claude Code" is rgba(20,40,69,0.2) over #383838 = #31353B, i.e.
   darker and slightly blue. Mine were lighter washes (#3D4450), which is how a low-alpha tint over grey
   ends up reading as grey with a hint of colour. And the rule matters as much as the fill — its
   "Setup Required" callout is #413D38 with a #5C4F3B border, and without that border the fill alone is
   just a smudge.

   ONE value per hue, everywhere it appears. The blue below is used by the Info card, the round's action
   block and every supporting panel — a colour picker moving between them must land on the same number,
   which it did not before (four different blues on one screen). */

.pl-card--critical {
    background-color: rgba(69, 24, 24, 0.30);
    border-color: rgba(224, 116, 116, 0.50);
    border-width: 1px;
}

.pl-card--warning {
    background-color: rgba(69, 52, 20, 0.30);
    border-color: rgba(228, 186, 104, 0.45);
    border-width: 1px;
}

.pl-card--info {
    background-color: rgba(20, 40, 69, 0.20);
    border-color: rgba(120, 174, 252, 0.40);
    border-width: 1px;
}

/* The same two values again — a caveat block and an Info card are different things, but "blue" is one
   colour in this window. */
/* Green completes the set. The verify screen's verdict is the one place a block can be POSITIVE, and it was
   the only tinted block left carrying its colour as a 3 px bar instead of as the block itself. */
.pl-card--good {
    background-color: rgba(24, 69, 40, 0.30);
    border-color: rgba(110, 214, 140, 0.45);
    border-width: 1px;
}

.pl-note--good {
    background-color: rgba(24, 69, 40, 0.30);
    border-color: rgba(110, 214, 140, 0.45);
    border-width: 1px;
    border-radius: 8px;
}

.pl-note--bad {
    background-color: rgba(69, 24, 24, 0.30);
    border-color: rgba(224, 116, 116, 0.50);
    border-width: 1px;
    border-radius: 8px;
}

.pl-note--warning {
    background-color: rgba(69, 52, 20, 0.30);
    border-color: rgba(228, 186, 104, 0.45);
    border-width: 1px;
    border-radius: 8px;
}

.pl-note--accent {
    background-color: rgba(20, 40, 69, 0.20);
    border-color: rgba(120, 174, 252, 0.40);
    border-width: 1px;
    border-radius: 8px;
}

/* ── buttons ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */

/* The one action a screen points at. Pale ink on a light accent, which reads as brighter than a saturated
   fill of the same area without shouting. */
.pl-primary {
    min-height: 32px;
    padding: 8px 16px;
    background-color: rgb(175, 232, 230);
    color: rgb(18, 20, 22);
    border-width: 0;
    border-radius: 8px;
    transition-duration: 200ms;
}

.pl-primary:hover {
    background-color: rgb(118, 231, 227);
    transition-duration: 200ms;
}

.pl-primary:active {
    background-color: rgb(250, 255, 234);
    transition-duration: 50ms;
}

/* Everything else you can click.

   The fill is an overlay, not a colour. It started as the reference's measured #464646, which is right on the
   window (#383838, +14) and nearly invisible on a card (helpbox is #404040, so +6 — Tim read the Locate button
   as "too dark", and measuring it agreed: the two buttons in that row have IDENTICAL fills, one just happens
   to be bold). These buttons sit on three different surfaces — the window, a plain card, and a severity-tinted
   card — so a fixed grey can only be correct on one of them. An overlay lifts by the same amount over all
   three, and picks up the tint when there is one.

   NEUTRAL GREY rather than white, and that is the one number here that changed when this stopped being one
   window's stylesheet. A white overlay only ever LIGHTENS. On the dark skin that is the direction you want; on
   the light one it lightens an already-light page and the button disappears — which did not matter while the
   only window wearing this was the Autopilot, and mattered immediately when the main panel's nine toolbar
   buttons stopped being stock editor buttons (those are correct on both skins) and started wearing this.

   127-grey goes the right way on both: it lifts a dark surface and darkens a light one. The alphas below are
   picked to land on the values the white overlay already produced on the dark skin, so nothing visibly moves
   there — over #383838 (56): 56 + a·71, so 0.28 → 76, which is the 0.10 white overlay's 76. Hover 0.51 → 92
   (was 92), active 0.73 → 108 (was 108), rule 0.45 → 88 (was 88). On the light skin (~#C8C8C8, 200) the same
   alphas give 180 / 163 / 147 with a 167 rule — a button you can see, which is the whole point.

   The idiom is not new here: .pl-tab below has always used a neutral grey, for exactly this reason. This class
   was the one place still doing it the other way.

   Geometry is still the reference's: rule #646464, radius 6, 30 high, 12 px label at #C8C8C8 — Install,
   Help/Talk and Check are all exactly that one button.

   min-height rather than height: their labels are one word and ours are sentences ("Measure anyway — checks
   nothing broke · about 70s"), and a fixed 30 would clip the second line off.

   The :hover rules are the entire point of this class existing. A Button that sets background-color in C#
   loses BOTH its USS hover and the editor's own built-in one — which is why "Open the full panel" had no
   highlight at all: it was styled inline from the day it was written. */
.pl-secondary {
    min-height: 30px;
    padding: 6px 12px;
    background-color: rgba(127, 127, 127, 0.28);
    color: var(--unity-colors-default-text);
    border-width: 1px;
    border-color: rgba(127, 127, 127, 0.45);
    border-radius: 6px;
    transition-duration: 200ms;
}

.pl-secondary:hover {
    background-color: rgba(127, 127, 127, 0.51);
    border-color: rgba(127, 127, 127, 0.62);
    transition-duration: 200ms;
}

.pl-secondary:active {
    background-color: rgba(127, 127, 127, 0.73);
    transition-duration: 50ms;
}

/* ── the step strip ───────────────────────────────────────────────────── */

/* The three steps, as a segmented control with a live hover.
 *
 * Their colours moved here for the same reason every other control's did: a background set in C# outranks
 * the :hover rule, so the strip was the last thing in the window that did not answer the mouse. Which step
 * is current is a class rather than an inline fill, because only a class can also carry a hover state. */
.pl-tab {
    background-color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0);
    color: var(--unity-colors-helpbox-text);
    border-width: 0;
    border-radius: 7px;
    transition-duration: 150ms;
}

.pl-tab:hover {
    background-color: rgba(127, 127, 127, 0.14);
    color: var(--unity-colors-default-text);
    transition-duration: 150ms;
}

.pl-tab--on {
    background-color: rgba(127, 127, 127, 0.24);
    color: var(--unity-colors-default-text);
}

.pl-tab--on:hover {
    background-color: rgba(127, 127, 127, 0.30);
}

/* A compact action, for the buttons that hang off a finding rather than off the screen.
 *
 * Theirs is height 20 / radius 4 / no border / 11 px — deliberately half the weight of a standalone button,
 * which is what keeps a card's actions from competing with the card's own title. Ours keeps 12 px (the floor
 * this window holds everywhere) and min-height, since our labels are phrases rather than single words. */
.pl-compact {
    min-height: 22px;
    padding: 2px 8px;
    /* The same neutral overlay as .pl-secondary, and for the same reason — see the note there. These are the
       buttons ON a finding, so they also have to hold up over a severity-tinted card, not just over the window. */
    background-color: rgba(127, 127, 127, 0.28);
    color: var(--unity-colors-default-text);
    border-width: 0;
    border-radius: 4px;
    font-size: 12px;
    /* Bold — all of them, which is the point.
     *
     * Not decoration: the shared card UI used to mark only the Fix button bold, so on a row with two actions one
     * was Normal and one Bold at the same size, on the same fill, with the same text colour — and measuring
     * proved that was the ONLY difference between them (both enabled, both opacity 1). Side by side, the Normal
     * one reads as disabled; Tim called it "wrong" twice before we found it. One tier of button, one look. Which
     * action is the consequential one is said by the word on it, not by its weight. PerfLintStyle.AsCompact now
     * clears any inline weight on the way in, because an inline value would outrank this line. */
    -unity-font-style: bold;
    transition-duration: 200ms;
}

.pl-compact:hover {
    background-color: rgba(127, 127, 127, 0.51);
    transition-duration: 200ms;
}

.pl-compact:active {
    background-color: rgba(127, 127, 127, 0.73);
    transition-duration: 50ms;
}

/* ── the same button, at toolbar density ──────────────────────────────── */

/* Geometry only — the fill, the rule and all three states come from .pl-secondary, which this is always worn
 * with. Nothing about the LOOK differs, and that is deliberate: two button appearances in one product is the
 * thing this file exists to stop.
 *
 * It exists because .pl-secondary is sized for a sentence standing on its own line, and the main panel's
 * toolbar is nine buttons on one row that does not wrap. At 30 high with 12 px of side padding that row grew
 * by 4 px and every button by 8, which is enough to push "Free / Pro" off the right-hand edge on a narrow
 * dock — the toolbar clips rather than wraps. Measured against the row it replaces (26 high), not guessed. */
.pl-secondary--dense {
    min-height: 26px;
    padding: 3px 9px;
}

/* ── a primary that is also a stop ────────────────────────────────────── */

/* Same box as .pl-primary, so a button that switches between them does not move by a pixel — only the fill
 * changes. The Runtime panel is the case: one button is "Start Sampling" and then "Stop & Analyze", and the
 * second has to read as something already running that you can end. A pale mint cannot say that.
 *
 * Deliberately not the severity red used for a Critical finding (rgba(224,116,116)) — that one means "this is
 * wrong", and a recording in progress is not wrong. */
.pl-danger {
    min-height: 32px;
    padding: 8px 16px;
    background-color: rgb(198, 86, 86);
    color: rgb(252, 244, 244);
    border-width: 0;
    border-radius: 8px;
    transition-duration: 200ms;
}

.pl-danger:hover {
    background-color: rgb(214, 102, 102);
    transition-duration: 200ms;
}

.pl-danger:active {
    background-color: rgb(232, 128, 128);
    transition-duration: 50ms;
}

/* ── a small inert pill ───────────────────────────────────────────────── */

/* A keyboard shortcut, a mode, a count — something to read, never something to click, which is why it has no
 * hover. A neutral grey at low alpha rather than the white overlay these were written with: white lightens on
 * a dark skin and washes out on a light one, so the chip was invisible on exactly one of the two. */
.pl-chip {
    background-color: rgba(127, 127, 127, 0.18);
    border-radius: 4px;
    padding: 1px 6px;
    font-size: 10px;
}
